	<section class="dh-wrap" id="impulse" aria-labelledby="h-imp">
		<div class="tr-imp">
			<div class="tr-imp-head">
				<div class="dh-stack" style="gap:18px">
					<p class="dh-kicker">Prompts for reflection · a few minutes a day</p>
					<h2 class="dh-h2" id="h-imp">Three prompts for your notebook.</h2>
				</div>
				<p>You do not need a programme, just a notebook or your notes app. Take a few minutes in the evening and write a line on one of the prompts. The point is not to produce punchlines but to look more closely.</p>
			</div>
			<ol class="tr-imp-list">
				<li class="tr-imp-card">
					<span class="tr-imp-nr n1" aria-hidden="true">1</span>
					<p class="tr-imp-tag">Observing</p>
					<h3>Train your inner <span class="tr-tip tr-tip--ende"><button type="button" class="tr-tip-btn" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="tip-seinfeld">Seinfeld</button><span class="tr-tip-box" id="tip-seinfeld" role="note"><b>Seinfeld</b> Popular US sitcom from the 1990s that turned small everyday observations into great comedy. A classic of observational humour.</span></span></h3>
					<p>Observational humour often starts with “You know when …?”. It is about things that are actually absurd but seem perfectly normal to us out of habit, or moments that lots of other people recognise too. If you develop a feel for this, you will find it easier to spot something you can laugh about together.</p>
					<div class="tr-imp-note"><b><svg class="tr-pen" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4 20l1-4L16 5l3 3L8 19l-4 1z" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linejoin="round"/><path d="M14 7l3 3" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/></svg>Write down</b>A moment from today that could start with “You know when …?”.<i class="tr-lines" aria-hidden="true"></i></div>
					<p class="tr-imp-bsp dh-voice" data-voice><span class="mk">“You know when you make yourself a cup of tea in the morning and two hours later it is still exactly where you left it?</span> <span class="mk pt">Now at room temperature.”</span></p>
					<p class="tr-imp-fine">A longer article on this is in the works.</p>
				</li>
				<li class="tr-imp-card">
					<span class="tr-imp-nr n2" aria-hidden="true">2</span>
					<p class="tr-imp-tag">Ambiguity</p>
					<h3>What else could it mean?</h3>
					<p>Many words and sentences have a second, hidden reading alongside the obvious one. In everyday life we hardly notice, because we automatically pick the obvious meaning. If you consciously notice ambiguity, you collect material for surprising connections, the same principle that many puns and jokes rely on.</p>
					<div class="tr-imp-note"><b><svg class="tr-pen" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4 20l1-4L16 5l3 3L8 19l-4 1z" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linejoin="round"/><path d="M14 7l3 3" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/></svg>Write down</b>A sentence you heard or said today, with its obvious reading and a hidden one.<i class="tr-lines" aria-hidden="true"></i></div>
					<p class="tr-imp-bsp dh-voice" data-voice><span class="mk">“Give me a ring later.” Obvious: a phone call.</span> <span class="mk pt">Hidden: a rather hasty marriage proposal.</span></p>
				</li>
				<li class="tr-imp-card">
					<span class="tr-imp-nr n3" aria-hidden="true">3</span>
					<p class="tr-imp-tag">Reframing</p>
					<h3>Reinterpret and reframe</h3>
					<p>A situation stays as it is. But you can try telling it differently: what is also funny, useful or surprising about it? That does not mean smiling your annoyance away, but placing a second view alongside it.</p>
					<div class="tr-imp-note"><b><svg class="tr-pen" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4 20l1-4L16 5l3 3L8 19l-4 1z" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linejoin="round"/><path d="M14 7l3 3" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/></svg>Write down</b>Something that went wrong today, and a second version of it.<span class="tr-imp-bonus"><b>Bonus point:</b> link the reframing to something you can be grateful for.</span><i class="tr-lines" aria-hidden="true"></i></div>
					<p class="tr-imp-bsp dh-voice" data-voice><span class="mk">“I stepped in dog poo today.</span> <span class="mk pt">Brilliant, I had been meaning to clean my trainers for ages anyway.”</span></p>
				</li>
			</ol>
			<div class="tr-imp-foot">
				<p class="dh-voice" data-voice><span class="mk">A note is not yet a punchline.</span> <span class="mk pt">It is material for later and, above all, training for your mental flexibility.</span></p>
				<a class="dh-link" href="<?php dehumore_href( '/2026/05/21/humor-trainieren-warum-dein-gehirn-dabei-mehr-lernt-als-beim-naechsten-mindset-seminar/' ); ?>" hreflang="de">Read more about training (in German)</a>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="dh-section dh-wrap" aria-labelledby="h-was">
		<div class="tr-head">
			<p class="dh-kicker dh-rise">What does humour training mean?</p>
			<h2 class="dh-h2 dh-rise" id="h-was">Not being funny on command. Discovering more possibilities in everyday life.</h2>
		</div>
		<div class="tr-what">
			<div class="tr-prose dh-rise">
				<p>Humour training does not start with telling jokes but with looking more closely: what is unexpected right now? What other reading is there? Humour is individual and depends on context; there is no formula for every situation. What you can practise, though, is perceiving more precisely, finding further interpretations and deciding more consciously whether humour fits the moment.</p>
			</div>
			<aside class="tr-aside dh-rise">
				<b>Reframing does not mean sugar-coating.</b>
				<p>Reframing means discovering further perspectives. It replaces neither acknowledging a problem nor a necessary solution.</p>
			</aside>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="dh-wrap" aria-labelledby="h-logik">
		<div class="tr-daily">
			<div class="dh-stack" style="gap:20px">
				<p class="dh-kicker dh-rise">How the training works</p>
				<h2 class="dh-h2 dh-rise" id="h-logik">Observe. Collect possibilities. Draw on them when the moment comes.</h2>
				<p class="dh-rise">If you practise this regularly, over time you will have more ideas to choose from. It is no guarantee of quick wit, but a way to have more options available, in everyday life, in presentations and in your team.</p>
				<a class="dh-link dh-rise" href="<?php dehumore_href( '/humor-in-organisationen/' ); ?>">Bring humour training into your team</a>
			</div>
			<ol class="tr-steps">
				<li class="dh-rise"><div><b>Observe everyday life</b><span>What was unexpected, contradictory or a little absurd today?</span></div></li>
				<li class="dh-rise"><div><b>Collect possibilities</b><span>What second, hidden reading sits alongside the obvious one? Write it down, even if it is not yet a punchline.</span></div></li>
				<li class="dh-rise"><div><b>Draw on them when the moment is right</b><span>Whether you share an idea is something you decide in the moment: who it suits, when and for what purpose.</span></div></li>
			</ol>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="dh-section dh-wrap" aria-labelledby="h-stress">
		<div class="tr-stress">
			<div class="dh-stack" style="gap:20px">
				<p class="dh-kicker dh-rise">Humour, stress and resilience</p>
				<h2 class="dh-h2 dh-rise" id="h-stress">Can humour help you deal with pressure differently?</h2>
				<div class="tr-prose dh-rise">
					<p>Humour can help you gain some distance from a situation. The situation stays the same; the way you look at it changes. That does not mean humour solves problems or makes pressures disappear.</p>
				</div>
				<div class="tr-shift dh-rise" aria-label="Example of gaining distance through a humorous view">
					<div><span>First thought</span><p>“Cold again. I can’t get anything done today.”</p></div>
					<div class="to"><span>Humorous view</span><p>“My tea has finally reached room temperature.”</p></div>
					<small>The annoyance can stay. The second view creates a little distance from the situation.</small>
				</div>
				<div class="tr-prose dh-rise">
					<p>Research on this uses very different methods. What was found with one method cannot simply be transferred to another. De:Humore sees humour as one possible resource that complements the coping strategies people already use. It does not replace necessary support, problem-solving or treatment. <span class="ref">(<a href="#q4">Martin &amp; Ford, 2018</a>)</span></p>
				</div>
			</div>
			<div class="tr-diff">
				<p class="dh-kicker dh-rise">Which method shows what</p>
				<div class="dh-rise"><b>Humorous reappraisal in the lab</b><p><span class="tr-tag bef">Finding</span>Participants were asked to reinterpret distressing images humorously. Afterwards, they reported fewer negative feelings in the short term than without this reinterpretation, especially with good-natured rather than mocking humour. <span class="ref">(<a href="#q6">Samson &amp; Gross, 2012</a>; <a href="#q7">Kugler &amp; Kuhbandner, 2015</a>)</span> The studies looked at brief lab situations, not everyday stress.</p></div>
				<div class="dh-rise"><b>Interventions that trigger laughter</b><p><span class="tr-tag bef">Finding</span>Such as laughter yoga, laughter exercises or comedy films. One meta-analysis finds indications of positive effects on psychological well-being. <span class="ref">(<a href="#q1">van der Wal &amp; Kok, 2019</a>)</span> Another found a greater decrease in the stress hormone cortisol after interventions involving spontaneous laughter than in control groups. <span class="ref">(<a href="#q2">Kramer &amp; Leitão, 2023</a>)</span> The studies vary widely in method and quality.</p></div>
				<div class="dh-rise"><b>Humour as a daily practice</b><p><span class="tr-tag off">Open</span>Noticing the funny side of things and reframing situations, as with the prompts above. Programmes that practise such habits are being studied. <span class="ref">(<a href="#q3">Ruch et al., 2018</a>)</span> Their effect on stress and resilience has not yet been researched much.</p></div>
				<div class="no-card dh-rise"><b>Specific promises of effects</b><p><span class="tr-tag no">Not supported</span>We do not promise that any particular humour training reduces stress, increases resilience or protects against burnout.</p></div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="dh-wrap" aria-labelledby="h-stil">
		<div class="tr-pair">
			<div class="tr-card light dh-rise">
				<p class="dh-kicker">Humour, personality and your own style</p>
				<h2 id="h-stil">You do not have to be funny the way someone else is.</h2>
				<p>People differ in the kind of humour they like, how they use humour and the situations in which they feel comfortable with it. So training should not tie everyone down to the same style.</p>
				<p>Humour styles and personality models can serve as tools for reflection and conversation. <span class="ref">(<a href="#q5">Martin et al., 2003</a>)</span> They help people talk about preferences and differences. They are not a diagnosis and do not reliably predict how a particular person will react in every situation.</p>
				<a class="dh-link" href="<?php dehumore_href( '/humor-und-persoenlichkeit/' ); ?>">The four humour styles in everyday life</a>
			</div>
			<div class="tr-card peach dh-rise">
				<p class="dh-kicker" style="color:var(--ink)">Humour needs context</p>
				<h2 id="h-kontext">Even a good idea needs the right moment.</h2>
				<p>A humorous idea does not work independently of the situation. Relationship, group norms, power relations, occasion and audience all influence how a joke may come across. Using humour deliberately therefore also means thinking about the situation, and having the freedom to do without humour.</p>
				<ul>
					<li>Who is involved, and who might feel targeted or left out?</li>
					<li>What do I want to achieve with this humour?</li>
					<li>Is now the right moment for it?</li>
				</ul>
				<div class="dh-actions"><a class="dh-btn" href="<?php dehumore_href( '/humor-risk-check/#check' ); ?>">Start the Humour Risk Check</a><a class="dh-link" href="<?php dehumore_href( '/2025/07/24/warum-mehr-humor-nicht-automatisch-besser-ist/' ); ?>" hreflang="de">When humour can be hurtful (in German)</a></div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

