	<section class="dh-section dh-wrap" aria-labelledby="h-faq">
		<div class="tr-head">
			<p class="dh-kicker dh-rise">Frequently asked questions</p>
			<h2 class="dh-h2 dh-rise" id="h-faq">Good to know</h2>
		</div>
		<div class="tr-faq">
			<details><summary>Do I have to be funny already to train my humour?</summary><div>No. You do not need to be able to come up with punchlines on the spot. A good start can be to perceive everyday situations more closely, develop other perspectives and get to know your own sense of humour better.</div></details>
			<details><summary>Does humour training mean always having the right joke ready?</summary><div>No. It is not about being funny in every situation. Regular practice can broaden your repertoire of observations and perspectives. Whether and when you share any of it is something you decide in each context.</div></details>
			<details><summary>Can humour really be trained?</summary><div>What can be trained includes perception, switching perspectives, developing alternative readings and reflecting on your own impact. That does not mean humour can be planned or guaranteed at any time.</div></details>
			<details><summary>Does humour help against stress?</summary><div>For some people, humour can be a way to gain distance or see a situation differently. Studies on interventions that trigger laughter and lab studies on humorous reappraisal provide indications of short-term relief. <span class="ref">(<a href="#q1">van der Wal &amp; Kok, 2019</a>; <a href="#q2">Kramer &amp; Leitão, 2023</a>; <a href="#q6">Samson &amp; Gross, 2012</a>)</span> But it does not follow that every humour training reduces stress or that humour replaces necessary support.</div></details>
			<details><summary>Does humour make you resilient?</summary><div>Humour is being studied as one possible resource for dealing with pressure. Resilience, however, is complex and depends on many factors. This page therefore does not promise that humour training automatically increases resilience.</div></details>
			<details><summary>Is it about seeing problems positively?</summary><div>No. Switching perspectives does not mean playing problems down or skipping over difficult feelings. It can open up an additional point of view when that suits the situation.</div></details>
			<details><summary>Is humour training suitable for teams too?</summary><div>Yes. Teams can reflect together on the role humour plays in their communication, how different reactions arise and which options suit their particular working context.</div></details>
		</div>
	</section>

